# The explainer
A page about FizzBuzz:  how it works, where it comes from, and its results, drawn as the counting sends them.
It's FizzBuzz's second listener, beside the console:  one algorithm, two listeners.
- It's off in the project file, so the example runs as a plain program, printing to the console.
- Turn it on (its import's `active`) and the same counting fills this page too.

## A result
One number the counting sent, and the word it got.

<spell-thing "result">
	<property "number" type="number"/>
	<property "word" description="fizz, buzz or fizzbuzz;  empty for a plain number">text</>
	<property "label" description="What its label says:  its word, or the number itself">
		the number as text if the word is ""
		the word otherwise
	</property>
	<property "color" description="Fizz blue, buzz green, both purple">
		"purple" if the word is "fizzbuzz"
		"blue" if the word is "fizz"
		"green" if the word is "buzz"
		"grey" otherwise
	</property>
</spell-thing>

<spell-thing "tally" template="list of results" description="Every result so far, a label each, in a row that wraps">
	<draw>
		<for result in it as="stack" direction="horizontal" wrap spacing="xs">
			<label color={the color of the result} appearance="basic">{the label of the result}</label>
		</for>
	</draw>
</spell-thing>

## The page

<spell-page "fizzbuzz explainer">
	<property "tally">a new tally</>
	<property "fizzes">the number of (results in the tally where its word is "fizz")</>
	<property "buzzes">the number of (results in the tally where its word is "buzz")</>
	<property "fizzbuzzes">the number of (results in the tally where its word is "fizzbuzz")</>
	<property "plain numbers">the number of (results in the tally where its word is "")</>

	<draw description="How it works, its roots, then the results with a count of each word">
		<container text>
			<stack>
				<header level="1">FizzBuzz</header>
				<section label="How it works">
					<!-- prettier-ignore -->
					<markdown>
					Count from 1 to 100, and say a word in place of some of the numbers:

					- a number 3 goes into exactly:  **fizz**
					- a number 5 goes into exactly:  **buzz**
					- a number both go into, which is any number 15 goes into:  **fizzbuzz**
					- every other number:  the number itself

					The order of the checks matters:  15 is checked first, because 15 is a fizz and a buzz too.

					This example's counting doesn't print anything.
					It sends an event for each number, `fizz`, `buzz`, `fizzbuzz` or `plain number`, carrying the number,
					and whoever is listening decides what to do with it:
					the console prints a line, and this page adds a label.
					</markdown>
				</section>
				<section label="Where it comes from">
					<!-- prettier-ignore -->
					<markdown>
					FizzBuzz began as a children's counting game in Britain, played to practise division:
					players count in turn, and anyone who says a number instead of "fizz" or "buzz" is out.

					It became famous among programmers in early 2007, as a test of whether someone can write code at all.
					Imran Ghory suggested it as a quick screen for job interviews,
					and Jeff Atwood's post "Why Can't Programmers.. Program?" spread the idea widely.
					Since then it has been written in nearly every programming language.
					</markdown>
				</section>
				<section label="The results">
					<statistics widths="4" size="mini">
						<statistic value={the fizzes} label="fizz" color="blue"/>
						<statistic value={the buzzes} label="buzz" color="green"/>
						<statistic value={the fizzbuzzes} label="fizzbuzz" color="purple"/>
						<statistic value={the plain numbers} label="plain numbers"/>
					</statistics>
					{draw the tally}
				</section>
			</stack>
		</container>
	</draw>
</spell-page>

## Listening
The page's own listener:  each event the counting sends becomes a result on the page.

<spell>
	to show (word as text) for (number as a number):
		add (a new result with number = number, word = word) to the tally of fizzbuzz explainer

	on fizzbuzz with number as a number:
		show "fizzbuzz" for number
	on fizz with number as a number:
		show "fizz" for number
	on buzz with number as a number:
		show "buzz" for number
	on plain number with number as a number:
		show "" for number
</spell>
